Leave a session's VT in graphics mode rather than text

greetd set each session's VT to text before starting it. When the boot
splash has just let go of the display, text mode makes the kernel put
its console back on the screen, at the console's own size, until the
session's compositor opens the GPU: on a laptop docked with its lid
shut, a box sized for the panel in the monitor's top left for seconds.

Graphics mode leaves the splash's last frame up instead, as other login
managers do. logind sets the VT back to text when the session's
controller lets go of it, so a text program started after a compositor
has gone, as a fallback greeter is, still has a console to write on.

diff --git a/greetd/src/session/worker.rs b/greetd/src/session/worker.rs
index 71c9d10..bbecfef 100644
--- a/greetd/src/session/worker.rs
+++ b/greetd/src/session/worker.rs
@@ -176,10 +176,11 @@ fn worker(sock: &UnixDatagram) -> Result<(), Error> {
             // Opening our target terminal.
             let target_term = terminal::Terminal::open(&path)?;
 
-            // Set the target VT mode to text for compatibility. Other login managers
-            // set this to graphics, but that disallows start of textual applications,
-            // which greetd aims to support.
-            target_term.kd_setmode(terminal::KdMode::Text)?;
+            // Set the target VT mode to graphics, as other login managers do: in text
+            // mode the kernel puts its console back on the screen while nothing holds
+            // the display, until the session's compositor takes it. logind sets the VT
+            // back to text once that compositor lets go of it.
+            target_term.kd_setmode(terminal::KdMode::Graphics)?;
 
             // Clear TTY so that it will be empty when we switch to it.
             target_term.term_clear()?;
